/**
\defgroup DICOMModule The DICOM Module
\ingroup MITKModules

\brief DICOM This module contains a number of classes for DICOM image loading within MITK.

\section DICOMModule_overview Scope

When people around MITK talk about loading DICOM images or series, they are really not interested in the
images (2D slices) but in a 3D mitk::Image instance that groups DICOM images in a meaningful way.

The task of this module is to provide
 - an interface for the general "DICOM files to mitk::Images" transformation: mitk::DICOMFileReader
 - a flexible default reader for the most common DICOM images: mitk::DICOMITKSeriesGDCMReader

Most documentation is kept with these classes, so please have a look at their documentations.

\remark Prior to this module, a class called DicomSeriesReader grew too much to be maintainable anymore. Its functionality is conserved by class mitk::ClassicDICOMSeriesReader.

\section DICOMModule_interface Module Interface

The general loading proceduce is structured by mitk::DICOMFileReader, under the assumption that the best reader for a set of files is not known prior to inspection.
To facilitate applications that need to select between possible readers based on what would be loaded,
file readers are required to implement loading in two separate steps:

 1. analysis of a list of files and description of potential mitk::Image%s by means of mitk::DICOMImageBlockDescriptor
 2. actual loading of pixel data into mitk::Image%s

A very simple implementation is mitk::DICOMFileReaderSelector which selects the reader with the least possible number of mitk::Images (least confusing for the user?).

\section DICOMModule_multiframe Multi-frame objects

A multi-frame file holds several pixel frames in one instance. MITK treats two
kinds differently, and the difference is whether the file carries a Per-Frame
Functional Groups Sequence (5200,9230) with one item per frame.

\subsection DICOMModule_multiframe_model The frame model

For a file that has one per-frame functional-group item per frame (Enhanced PET,
Enhanced CT, Enhanced MR, Legacy Converted Enhanced, and any other SOP class
built the same way; single-frame instances of them included), the reader creates
one mitk::DICOMImageFrameInfo per frame, with FrameNo the stored frame index.
Files remain the unit of sorting and frames are never reordered.

Every attribute inside a functional group then appears as one
mitk::TemporoSpatialStringProperty per attribute, named by the path of the
attribute relative to the functional-group item, with one value per (t, z) slot:

\code
DICOM.0028.9145.[0].0028.1053     Pixel Value Transformation, Rescale Slope
DICOM.0020.9111.[0].0018.9151     Frame Content, Frame Reference DateTime
\endcode

The (5200,9229) or (5200,9230) root is not part of the key. Whether the encoder
put a macro in the shared or in the per-frame group is an encoding choice a
consumer should not have to know: both placements yield the same key, a shared
value simply repeating at every slot. The item index of the macro sequence is
kept, because a macro may carry several items for one frame (Real World Value
Mapping routinely does) and a path entering a sequence without naming an item is
not addressable.

The value at a slot and the pixels at that slot always come from the same frame.
Today every frame k occupies slot (0, k), because GDCM stacks all frames of a
file along z whatever the Dimension Index attributes say. The stored frame index
of each slot is recorded in MITK.IO.reader.DICOM.frames, so the mapping stays
recoverable from the loaded image; the files property repeats one filename for
all slots of a multi-frame file and cannot express it.

\subsection DICOMModule_multiframe_pixels Pixel values

The Pixel Value Transformation is applied per frame. GDCM's gdcm::Image can hold
only one slope and intercept and applies the pair of the first per-frame item to
the whole buffer, so the reader corrects the frames whose own pair differs. The
component type follows GDCM's own rule applied over all frames' pairs, so a file
with a uniform transformation and one with a varying transformation load with the
same type.

Real World Value Mapping is not applied. GDCM does not apply it either, and it
remains a consumer concern.

\subsection DICOMModule_multiframe_limits What is not covered

 - A file whose per-frame item count does not match its frame count cannot be
   mapped to slots. It keeps the one-frame model, publishes its values under the
   rooted keys as before, and the reader says so once per scan.
 - A file without functional groups (RT Dose, multi-frame NM, SC, US) keeps the
   one-frame model as well. Its frames load as slices and no per-frame value is
   available, which is normal rather than an error; mitkDICOMVolumeDiagnostics
   reports it as an informational finding.
 - Geometry comes from GDCM. An object whose frames are not stored in spatial
   order keeps GDCM's unit-spacing fallback.
 - A file with a frame model is never combined with another file in one block,
   because ITK's multi-file branch sets the moving dimension to the file count
   and would keep one frame per file. Such a block is separated into one volume
   per file, each flagged mitk::IOVolumeSplitReason::ReasonType::MultiFrameFileSeparated.
   Note that a conformant functional-group object carries Image Position
   (Patient) in the Plane Position macro and nothing at the top level, so the
   sorters already give it a block of its own; the separation matters for
   non-conformant files that carry top-level geometry as well.
 - Frame-aware sorting or splitting (Dimension Index Values, In-Stack Position,
   Temporal Position Index), 3D+t decomposition of one file, and Segmentation
   and Parametric Map objects are not covered. FrameNo is the hook for the
   first of these.

\section DICOMModule_tasks Tasks for future development

Unstructured development tasks and ideas for future extensions

 - frame-aware sorting and splitting, so that the frames of one file can form
   several volumes or a 3D+t image
 - per-frame geometry for objects whose frames are not stored in spatial order
 - perspective: slice-by-slice loading

*/
